WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:06.480
Whereas writing to a file infers that we are&nbsp;
writing to a new file or overwriting an existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:14.160
file, the append command adds text to the end&nbsp;
of an existing file. We have established that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.800
in gaming, one or more configuration files&nbsp;
may be needed to store essential information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:25.960
such as a player’s statistics. For this append&nbsp;
example, we will use a variation of the file we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:36.000
have used throughout this section. This version,&nbsp;
315-append, will use a file named 315-message.txt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:44.800
In the first four lines, we open the file in&nbsp;
write mode, write two statements to the file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:52.480
and then close the file. Let’s say that some time&nbsp;
has passed in the game, and we need to append some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:01:01.800
text to the end of this text file. First, let’s&nbsp;
ensure any new text goes onto a new line. On line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:09.640
three, I will add a backslash and n inside the&nbsp;
quoted text to indicate the want for a new line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:19.080
after this text is added to the text file. Next,&nbsp;
and this is a small example of reusing code, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:30.600
is always preferable to retyping code, I will copy&nbsp;
the open statement from line 1 to line 6. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:43.760
I will change the mode from w to a for append.&nbsp;
As is the case with the r and w modes, a plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:51.400
sign indicates opening the file in read and write&nbsp;
mode. On the next line of code, line 7, I will add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.400 --> 00:02:05.840
a write statement to add the player name to the&nbsp;
text file. Then, on line 8, I will copy down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:16.640
close function to close the file. So that we can&nbsp;
see this code work properly, in two stages, I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:28.480
comment out the last three lines of code. We will&nbsp;
cover commenting in more detail elsewhere in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:34.560
course, but adding number symbols to the beginning&nbsp;
of a line tells Python to ignore the line. I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:49.440
run the code, and the 315-message text file is&nbsp;
built. In opening the file, we see the two lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:58.760
of text from lines 2 and 3 in the code. To run the&nbsp;
append portion of the code to mimic a real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:11.920
situation, I will add number symbols to the&nbsp;
first four lines to have Python ignore that code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:28.360
and then I will uncomment lines 6 through 8 and&nbsp;
run that code. Then, I will open the file again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:34.520
and the append worked as text has been added&nbsp;
to the end of the file. Remember that regarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:45.960
existing files, write overwrites existing files&nbsp;
while append adds to the end of existing files.

